{"name":"Minified Malachite","hex":"#ddf3e5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"minified","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of minify"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/minified"},{"term":"malachite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bright green mineral, a basic copper carbonate, Cu2CO3(OH)2; one of the principal ores of copper.","A mild green color, like that of the mineral."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a color ranging from olive-taupe to a mild to deeply-rich (at times seemingly translucent) green, like that of the mineral which is present on oxidized copper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/malachite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Minification (programming)","blocks":[{"text":"Minification is the process of removing all unnecessary characters from the source code of interpreted programming languages or markup languages without changing its functionality. These unnecessary characters usually include whitespace characters, new line characters, comments, and sometimes block delimiters, which are used to add readability to the code but are not required for it to execute. Minification reduces the size of the source code, making its transmission over a network more efficient. In programmer culture, aiming at extremely minified source code is the purpose of recreational code golf competitions and a part of the demoscene."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minification_(programming)","wikidata":"Q6865248"},{"title":"Malachite","blocks":[{"text":"Malachite is a copper carbonate hydroxide mineral, with the formula Cu2CO3(OH)2. This opaque, green-banded mineral crystallizes in the monoclinic crystal system, and most often forms botryoidal, fibrous, or stalagmitic masses, in fractures and deep, underground spaces, where the water table and hydrothermal fluids provide the means for chemical precipitation. Individual crystals are rare, but occur as slender to acicular prisms. Pseudomorphs after more tabular or blocky azurite crystals also occur."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Malachite%2C_Zaire.jpg/330px-Malachite%2C_Zaire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malachite","wikidata":"Q164411"},{"title":"Shades of green","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color green may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a green or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Shades_of_Green.svg/330px-Shades_of_Green.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_green","wikidata":"Q19847180"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Great_Orme_Copper_Mine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819.jpg/500px-Great_Orme_Copper_Mine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Great_Orme_Copper_Mine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819.jpg/500px-Great_Orme_Copper_Mine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The entrance to the Neolithic era malachite mine complex on the Great Orme, Wales","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreat_Orme_Copper_Mine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_819.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Outokumpu_malachite.jpg/500px-Outokumpu_malachite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Outokumpu_malachite.jpg/1280px-Outokumpu_malachite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&